1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,125
Imagine reading two conversations at once, interleaved line by
line.

2
00:00:04,305 --> 00:00:06,344
In one, the code for the door is seven.

3
00:00:06,524 --> 00:00:08,586
In the other, the code for the door is three.

4
00:00:08,766 --> 00:00:12,503
Later, someone from the first conversation asks: what was the
code?

5
00:00:13,867 --> 00:00:19,499
A memory that simply links door to whatever followed it has
stored both answers in the same place.

6
00:00:19,679 --> 00:00:22,866
The best it can do is guess, and it will be right half the time.

7
00:00:23,233 --> 00:00:24,622
One fix is obvious.

8
00:00:24,802 --> 00:00:28,993
Give the memory separate compartments, and file each
conversation in its own.

9
00:00:29,173 --> 00:00:34,133
Then the question reads only from the compartment it belongs to,
and the conflict is gone.

10
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:36,291
The hard part is the word give.

11
00:00:36,471 --> 00:00:40,038
Can a network discover, from the task alone, that it should
split its memory this way, and which tokens belong in which

12
00:00:40,038 --> 00:00:43,606
compartment?

13
00:00:44,767 --> 00:00:51,750
That is the question behind the repository Ynkling slash
clankers, and its technical report: Multi-Channel Hebbian

14
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:58,732
Plasticity in Multilayer BDH Solves Context-Conditional Binding
by Partitioning Memory.

15
00:00:58,912 --> 00:01:05,444
Its subtitle gives the news: an early-training recipe now finds
the partition without restarts in most runs, for two streams and

16
00:01:05,444 --> 00:01:11,976
for four with spare channels, while at eight streams the gate
forgets the context.

17
00:01:12,156 --> 00:01:16,188
This video covers all of it: the model, the task, the code, the
experiments, what works, what still fails, and how the work was

18
00:01:16,188 --> 00:01:20,221
checked.

19
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,591
The model underneath is BDH, the Dragon Hatchling, introduced by
Pathway in 2025.

20
00:01:31,771 --> 00:01:34,748
BDH keeps its working memory in synapses.

21
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:38,951
Each token activates a sparse set of neurons, and a Hebbian rule
strengthens the connections between neurons that are active

22
00:01:38,951 --> 00:01:42,802
together.

23
00:01:46,933 --> 00:01:51,871
Reading the memory means asking which stored patterns overlap
with what is active now.

24
00:01:52,233 --> 00:01:54,922
On a GPU this becomes linear attention.

25
00:01:55,102 --> 00:01:59,585
The score between the current position t and an earlier position
s is a content match: the dot product of their sparse, positive

26
00:01:59,585 --> 00:02:04,068
neuron codes.

27
00:02:04,248 --> 00:02:09,215
In this project a positional decay of 0.95 per token of distance
weights it, so a memory fades to half its strength in about

28
00:02:09,215 --> 00:02:14,181
fourteen tokens.

29
00:02:15,333 --> 00:02:18,793
The public implementation, bdh dot py, is short.

30
00:02:18,973 --> 00:02:23,928
Each layer encodes the residual stream into N neurons and keeps
the positive part.

31
00:02:24,108 --> 00:02:29,237
It attends with queries tied to keys and no softmax, normalizes
the result, passes it through a second sparse code, multiplies

32
00:02:29,237 --> 00:02:34,366
the two codes, and decodes back.

33
00:02:34,546 --> 00:02:36,294
The same weights serve every layer.

34
00:02:37,433 --> 00:02:42,343
The repository uses that file unmodified, at a tiny size: three
layers, two hundred fifty-six neurons, an embedding width of

35
00:02:42,343 --> 00:02:47,253
thirty-two, one head.

36
00:02:47,433 --> 00:02:53,949
About twenty-six thousand parameters, and twenty-eight to
twenty-nine thousand once the gate we meet next is added.

37
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:01,406
Here is the task the whole project is built on.

38
00:03:01,586 --> 00:03:06,089
There are S streams, each announced by its own context token,
and P keys.

39
00:03:06,269 --> 00:03:11,325
For every key, each stream gets its own value, drawn from
sixteen value tokens.

40
00:03:11,505 --> 00:03:14,560
So the same key means something different in every stream.

41
00:03:15,633 --> 00:03:19,195
The sequence is a list of triples: context, key, value.

42
00:03:19,375 --> 00:03:23,712
In the grouped layout, the triples for one key sit together; the
order of the keys is random, and so is the order of the streams

43
00:03:23,712 --> 00:03:28,048
within each key's group.

44
00:03:28,228 --> 00:03:32,864
At the end comes a query: a context token, a key, and a blank to
fill in.

45
00:03:33,233 --> 00:03:36,723
With two streams and four keys that is twenty-seven tokens.

46
00:03:36,903 --> 00:03:39,788
Here the query asks for key zero in stream zero.

47
00:03:39,968 --> 00:03:45,417
Key zero appears twice: value thirteen in stream one, and value
two in stream zero.

48
00:03:45,597 --> 00:03:47,065
The answer is value two.

49
00:03:48,467 --> 00:03:50,128
There are three reference levels.

50
00:03:50,308 --> 00:03:52,983
Guessing among the values gives one in sixteen.

51
00:03:53,163 --> 00:04:01,363
A model that binds keys to values but cannot tell the streams
apart has S candidates and scores one over S: here, one half.

52
00:04:01,543 --> 00:04:05,306
Only a model that resolves the conflict by stream reaches one.

53
00:04:05,667 --> 00:04:07,109
That middle level is a trap.

54
00:04:07,289 --> 00:04:13,078
A single memory stores both bindings of a key in the same place,
and in the basic setting, without the convolution we meet later,

55
00:04:13,078 --> 00:04:18,867
not one of more than two hundred single-channel runs escaped it.

56
00:04:23,333 --> 00:04:30,973
The extension gives every synapse k channels instead of one: k
separate Hebbian memories that share all the network's weights.

57
00:04:31,153 --> 00:04:36,223
Every token also gets a gate, a probability distribution over
the k channels.

58
00:04:36,403 --> 00:04:40,813
The gate spreads the token's write across the channels and
blends its read from them.

59
00:04:41,166 --> 00:04:45,956
With one distribution doing both jobs, the attention score gains
a second factor.

60
00:04:46,136 --> 00:04:48,486
The first is the content match from before.

61
00:04:48,666 --> 00:04:51,413
The second is the match between the two tokens' gates.

62
00:04:51,766 --> 00:04:57,581
If two tokens put their gates on the same channel, that factor
is one and they see each other's memory.

63
00:04:57,761 --> 00:05:02,170
If they use different channels it is zero, and they are
invisible to each other.

64
00:05:02,350 --> 00:05:08,009
So tokens that share a context share memory, and tokens from
different contexts are isolated.

65
00:05:08,189 --> 00:05:13,420
With k equal to one, the factor is always one, and the model is
plain BDH.

66
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:18,077
A hand-set perfect gate sends every token of stream s to channel
s.

67
00:05:18,257 --> 00:05:23,863
With it, the conflict disappears, and the model binds: ten of
ten runs in the basic setting.

68
00:05:24,233 --> 00:05:26,996
The real question is whether a gate can learn this.

69
00:05:27,176 --> 00:05:34,228
The learned gate, arm A, is a small recurrent network over the
token embeddings, with a thirty-two unit state h.

70
00:05:34,408 --> 00:05:39,734
At each step, h is the hyperbolic tangent of the sum of two
terms: the input term, which looks at the current token, and the

71
00:05:39,734 --> 00:05:45,060
recurrent term, which carries the previous state forward.

72
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:48,188
A linear map and a softmax turn h into the gate.

73
00:05:48,566 --> 00:05:51,803
Nothing tells this gate which stream a token belongs to.

74
00:05:51,983 --> 00:05:54,533
It has to find that out from the task loss alone.

75
00:05:54,713 --> 00:05:57,541
Keep its two terms in mind; they come back at the end.

76
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:06,117
Learning that is harder than it looks, and a short calculation
shows why.

77
00:06:06,297 --> 00:06:12,504
Write each gate as the uniform distribution plus a deviation,
delta, whose entries sum to zero.

78
00:06:12,684 --> 00:06:17,413
The gate match becomes one over k, plus the dot product of the
two deviations.

79
00:06:17,767 --> 00:06:23,505
The constant one over k scales every score alike, and the layer
norm after attention cancels it.

80
00:06:23,685 --> 00:06:26,722
So routing enters only through products of deviations.

81
00:06:26,902 --> 00:06:31,040
The gradient for one token's deviation is proportional to the
other tokens' deviations, and at the uniform gate those are all

82
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:35,177
zero.

83
00:06:35,357 --> 00:06:38,904
The task loss exerts no first-order pull toward any routing.

84
00:06:39,267 --> 00:06:44,705
And the gate does start almost exactly uniform, because the
embeddings are initialized small.

85
00:06:44,885 --> 00:06:48,924
At the first step, the gradient reaching the gate has a norm
below ten to the minus five; the rest of the model's is about

86
00:06:48,924 --> 00:06:52,962
2.6.

87
00:06:53,142 --> 00:06:59,019
One more detail: the gate ends in a softmax, so the k rows of
the gradient into its output matrix, W_g, sum to zero, and that

88
00:06:59,019 --> 00:07:04,896
gradient has rank at most k minus one.

89
00:07:05,267 --> 00:07:10,052
So the gate sits on a flat spot, and whatever pushes it off
first decides where it goes.

90
00:07:17,833 --> 00:07:19,103
Two more parts of the setup.

91
00:07:19,283 --> 00:07:23,932
Since Phase Four, most arms include a short causal convolution,
the kind Mamba and most modern linear-attention models place

92
00:07:23,932 --> 00:07:28,581
before their token mixer.

93
00:07:28,761 --> 00:07:32,868
It is a depthwise filter four tokens wide, applied at every
layer and initialized to the identity, so at first it changes

94
00:07:32,868 --> 00:07:36,975
nothing.

95
00:07:37,155 --> 00:07:42,633
It feeds the queries and the values; the residual stream and the
gate's input are not convolved.

96
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:48,557
In this task each context token sits two positions before its
value, so the convolution's lag-two weight can carry the context

97
00:07:48,557 --> 00:07:53,514
right onto the value's position.

98
00:07:53,694 --> 00:07:57,810
That gives even a single-channel model a second route to
binding, one that never separates the streams, and a gated model

99
00:07:57,810 --> 00:08:01,925
can take it too.

100
00:08:02,300 --> 00:08:07,024
That is why results with the convolution are scored on routing,
not on binding alone.

101
00:08:09,300 --> 00:08:10,696
Second, the optimizer.

102
00:08:10,876 --> 00:08:14,108
Before Phase Five every main-line test used Adam.

103
00:08:14,288 --> 00:08:15,732
Phase Five adds Muon.

104
00:08:15,912 --> 00:08:20,846
Muon takes the momentum of each weight matrix it handles and
replaces it by an approximately orthogonalized version, computed

105
00:08:20,846 --> 00:08:25,780
with five Newton–Schulz steps.

106
00:08:25,960 --> 00:08:30,677
As a result, the size of an update no longer depends on the size
of the gradient.

107
00:08:31,733 --> 00:08:36,899
Muon handles the weight matrices, each head slice of a
three-dimensional weight included.

108
00:08:37,079 --> 00:08:42,103
The embedding and the convolution stay with Adam, along with any
one-dimensional parameters.

109
00:08:47,067 --> 00:08:48,279
How is a run scored?

110
00:08:48,459 --> 00:08:52,141
A model trains at batch thirty-two for a fixed budget, usually
twenty-four thousand or twenty-eight thousand eight hundred

111
00:08:52,141 --> 00:08:55,824
steps.

112
00:08:56,004 --> 00:09:01,737
Every twelve hundred steps it is evaluated on two thousand and
forty-eight held-out queries, and it stops early after three

113
00:09:01,737 --> 00:09:07,470
evaluations in a row at or above 0.95.

114
00:09:07,834 --> 00:09:16,616
A run is bound if an evaluation reaches 0.95 and every later one
stays there; the first such step is its transition.

115
00:09:16,796 --> 00:09:20,631
With two streams and two channels, a run is discovered if it is
bound and the streams' read gates are separated at the value

116
00:09:20,631 --> 00:09:24,467
positions.

117
00:09:24,647 --> 00:09:30,546
With more than two streams and at least as many channels as
streams, a run is bound and routed if it is bound, every stream

118
00:09:30,546 --> 00:09:36,446
has a channel of its own, and every stream is at least ninety
percent accurate.

119
00:09:37,800 --> 00:09:40,503
Screens also score routing partway through a run.

120
00:09:40,683 --> 00:09:44,773
The routing margin compares how well the query's gate matches
its target value with how well it matches the same key's values

121
00:09:44,773 --> 00:09:48,864
in other streams.

122
00:09:49,044 --> 00:09:53,407
With a margin of at least 0.9, and over ninety percent of the
gate's variance explained by stream, a run counts as routed at

123
00:09:53,407 --> 00:09:57,770
that step.

124
00:09:59,434 --> 00:10:00,842
The failures have names.

125
00:10:01,022 --> 00:10:07,330
The code looks at the gate's channel choice at key positions,
and asks how much of its variance is explained by position,

126
00:10:07,330 --> 00:10:13,638
either the triple's index or which half of the sequence it is
in, or by the key's identity.

127
00:10:13,818 --> 00:10:15,357
That fraction is eta squared.

128
00:10:15,537 --> 00:10:19,317
At one half or more, a failed run is a POSITION or a KEY
failure.

129
00:10:21,300 --> 00:10:26,614
With more than two streams, a gate can also put several streams
in one channel: a merge.

130
00:10:26,794 --> 00:10:31,604
And a run whose final accuracy stays below 0.15 has collapsed.

131
00:10:31,784 --> 00:10:36,471
Keep these in mind; most of the story is about which of them a
gate falls into.

132
00:10:41,067 --> 00:10:45,201
This report is Revision Seven, and it stands on four earlier
phases.

133
00:10:45,567 --> 00:10:50,484
In Phase One the gate was a function of token identity alone,
and it could not express the routing, because which stream a key

134
00:10:50,484 --> 00:10:55,402
belongs to is not a property of the key.

135
00:10:55,582 --> 00:11:01,966
The lesson: check that a target is in the model's function class
before reading anything into a failure to learn it.

136
00:11:02,146 --> 00:11:06,580
In Phase Two a recurrent gate could express it, and it separated
two streams.

137
00:11:06,934 --> 00:11:08,833
Phase Three built the binding task.

138
00:11:09,013 --> 00:11:14,941
Two channels with the perfect gate bound two streams where no
single-channel model did, and the learned gate found the routing

139
00:11:14,941 --> 00:11:20,869
on about half its runs, beating every single-channel reference
on both machines.

140
00:11:21,234 --> 00:11:23,717
Phase Four ran ten pre-registered tests.

141
00:11:23,897 --> 00:11:28,830
A readout from the gate's state into the residual stream harmed
the gate, through its gradient.

142
00:11:29,010 --> 00:11:34,574
At two streams, more channels did not raise the gate's rate, but
a restart rule that reads only held-out accuracy at step

143
00:11:34,574 --> 00:11:40,138
twenty-four hundred succeeded in sixty of sixty trials.

144
00:11:40,318 --> 00:11:44,158
The short convolution did not replace the gate at the working
learning rate: one channel bound seven of eighty runs, the gate

145
00:11:44,158 --> 00:11:47,999
fifty-one.

146
00:11:48,179 --> 00:11:54,563
But at four times that rate, one channel with the convolution
bound thirty-four of eighty, through its lag-two weight.

147
00:11:56,734 --> 00:12:03,196
With eight keys per stream, at the working rate, the gate bound
from scratch in forty-nine of seventy runs, while one channel

148
00:12:03,196 --> 00:12:09,658
with the same fast-weight memory and nearly twice the parameters
bound none of twenty-four.

149
00:12:09,838 --> 00:12:12,519
The advantage is the partition, not the memory.

150
00:12:12,699 --> 00:12:19,310
At four streams with four channels the gate bound only fourteen
of forty, mostly failing by merging streams.

151
00:12:19,490 --> 00:12:22,930
Eight or sixteen channels removed most of those merges.

152
00:12:23,110 --> 00:12:27,371
What remained was an early commitment to a split by something
other than the stream.

153
00:12:32,533 --> 00:12:38,536
Before the new results, a word on how they were produced,
because the repository is organized around it.

154
00:12:38,716 --> 00:12:43,525
Every confirmatory experiment is one file: test underscore name
dot py.

155
00:12:43,705 --> 00:12:49,624
Its docstring is written before any run: the background, the
arms, the seeds, the claims and their thresholds, the checks,

156
00:12:49,624 --> 00:12:55,543
and a runtime projection with rules for what to cut if time runs
short.

157
00:12:57,200 --> 00:13:03,160
The test prints its verdicts mechanically, and after the run the
result is appended to the same docstring.

158
00:13:03,340 --> 00:13:08,497
Before training, each test runs a chain of numbered checks
inherited from earlier tests, more than a hundred by now,

159
00:13:08,497 --> 00:13:13,653
including reproducing a recorded run bit for bit.

160
00:13:15,400 --> 00:13:22,979
Two machines run every Phase Four and Phase Five test: X, a
cloud container, and L, an Intel laptop processor.

161
00:13:23,159 --> 00:13:25,045
Every verdict is given per machine.

162
00:13:25,225 --> 00:13:30,385
They run the same seeds, so pooled counts are descriptive, not
independent evidence.

163
00:13:30,565 --> 00:13:35,223
A third container, E, runs quick exploratory screens on a
separate branch, and nothing there counts as a result until a

164
00:13:35,223 --> 00:13:39,880
pre-registered test confirms it.

165
00:13:41,633 --> 00:13:43,141
Most comparisons are paired.

166
00:13:43,321 --> 00:13:47,266
A seed fixes the initial weights and the stream of training
batches, so two arms on the same seed differ only in the thing

167
00:13:47,266 --> 00:13:51,211
under test.

168
00:13:51,391 --> 00:13:57,935
The statistic is an exact McNemar test on the discordant seeds,
where one arm succeeded and the other failed.

169
00:13:58,115 --> 00:14:01,255
When you hear "eleven versus zero", that is what it counts.

170
00:14:01,435 --> 00:14:09,017
Results are also put in bands: reliable at ninety percent or
more, majority at half, minority for at least one success.

171
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:21,209
Here is the repository itself. bdh dot py is Pathway's model,
unmodified.

172
00:14:21,389 --> 00:14:27,255
The multi-channel model, MultiBDH, lives in test multilayer
binding dot py.

173
00:14:27,435 --> 00:14:35,169
It subclasses BDH and replaces only the attention module,
multiplying every score by the gate match at every layer.

174
00:14:35,349 --> 00:14:41,265
With the convolution on, it runs a copy of BDH's layer loop with
the convolution inserted.

175
00:14:41,445 --> 00:14:44,774
The gate's own code is imported from the Phase Two instrument.

176
00:14:45,133 --> 00:14:52,162
Then come the experiments, thirty-four test files in the order
the project ran them: the Phase One and Two instrument tests,

177
00:14:52,162 --> 00:14:59,191
the Phase Three binding tests, the ten Phase Four tests, and the
five Phase Five tests this report adds.

178
00:14:59,371 --> 00:15:04,274
Each later test imports its predecessors, so the chain of checks
grows with the project.

179
00:15:04,633 --> 00:15:09,586
Machine X's results live in results slash X: a JSON file for
each recorded test, and a table of the commit, processor and

180
00:15:09,586 --> 00:15:14,539
start time behind each one.

181
00:15:14,719 --> 00:15:19,562
Of machine L's Phase Five results, only the early-recipe file is
committed so far; the report regenerated L's other verdicts from

182
00:15:19,562 --> 00:15:24,405
L's own files.

183
00:15:24,767 --> 00:15:31,033
And mind the branches: at the time of this video, the default
branch's README still follows Revision Six, Revision Seven and

184
00:15:31,033 --> 00:15:37,299
its README sit on the research branch, and the screens live on a
branch of their own.

185
00:15:37,667 --> 00:15:39,375
Running a test is one command.

186
00:15:39,555 --> 00:15:44,899
It runs its checks, prints the projection, then trains on four
workers with one thread each.

187
00:15:45,079 --> 00:15:48,223
Finished runs are cached, so an interrupted test resumes.

188
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:55,382
Training is deterministic for a given processor and thread
count, which is what makes bit-for-bit pairing possible.

189
00:15:55,562 --> 00:15:59,993
A flag can pool another machine's results, and tests that pair
with earlier runs refuse to pair unless a recorded run

190
00:15:59,993 --> 00:16:04,423
reproduces exactly.

191
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:06,043
Two practical notes.

192
00:16:06,223 --> 00:16:12,136
The git log lets you audit the pre-registration, since each
test's design is committed before its result.

193
00:16:12,316 --> 00:16:19,148
And the tests load older modules straight from git history, so
run them from a git clone, not a downloaded archive.

194
00:16:23,600 --> 00:16:30,209
Phase Five adds five pre-registered tests, each run on both
machines, and thirteen batches of screens.

195
00:16:30,389 --> 00:16:32,222
The first asked about four streams.

196
00:16:33,500 --> 00:16:40,330
Revision Six had noticed, after the fact, that at four streams
with spare channels, a held-out accuracy of at least 0.4 at step

197
00:16:40,330 --> 00:16:47,160
forty-eight hundred had picked the eventual binders with
thirty-eight of thirty-eight precision.

198
00:16:47,340 --> 00:16:55,912
The stream recipe test made that a restart rule on fresh seeds:
four streams, four keys, the convolution; continue a run if it

199
00:16:55,912 --> 00:17:04,484
clears 0.4 at step forty-eight hundred, otherwise restart from a
new seed, up to five attempts, the fifth continuing regardless.

200
00:17:06,267 --> 00:17:09,312
It ran with sixteen channels, and with four as a control.

201
00:17:10,667 --> 00:17:16,404
With sixteen channels and restarts, seventeen of twenty bound on
X and nineteen of twenty on L.

202
00:17:16,584 --> 00:17:18,408
Without restarts, twelve and nine.

203
00:17:18,588 --> 00:17:23,207
With four channels, restarts barely helped: ten and eight,
against ten and five.

204
00:17:25,067 --> 00:17:26,517
The reason is merges.

205
00:17:26,697 --> 00:17:30,406
If two of the four streams share a channel, those two are each
right half the time, and the run's accuracy sits near three

206
00:17:30,406 --> 00:17:34,116
quarters.

207
00:17:34,296 --> 00:17:37,086
Three streams in one channel gives about one half.

208
00:17:37,266 --> 00:17:40,185
Both clear the 0.4 bar, and then stall.

209
00:17:40,365 --> 00:17:47,797
With four channels, every attempt that passed the check and then
failed was a merge: nine of nine on X, twelve of twelve on L.

210
00:17:48,167 --> 00:17:50,390
So the check works only with spare channels.

211
00:17:50,570 --> 00:17:58,963
The restart recipe was reliable on L and a majority on X, and
spare channels making restarts work was shown on both machines.

212
00:17:59,143 --> 00:18:04,589
Without restarts, sixteen channels did not bind significantly
more than four on either machine.

213
00:18:04,769 --> 00:18:10,976
L also tried eight streams: the perfect gate bound both its
runs, and the restart arm none of five.

214
00:18:15,733 --> 00:18:19,386
From scratch, eight streams with sixteen channels did not bind.

215
00:18:19,566 --> 00:18:23,720
So the stream curriculum test trained on two of the eight
streams per sequence for the first forty-eight hundred updates,

216
00:18:23,720 --> 00:18:27,874
then four, then all eight.

217
00:18:28,054 --> 00:18:31,652
The perfect gate bound on both machines, so the setup was valid.

218
00:18:32,033 --> 00:18:36,713
The curriculum bound six of sixteen runs on X and four of twenty
on L; training on all eight from the start bound none, zero of

219
00:18:36,713 --> 00:18:41,392
six and zero of ten.

220
00:18:41,572 --> 00:18:48,175
With so few runs, neither difference was significant, so "the
curriculum beats training from scratch" was not shown.

221
00:18:51,733 --> 00:18:55,470
The scratch runs mostly collapsed below 0.15.

222
00:18:55,650 --> 00:18:59,532
On X, every curriculum binder bound soon after the switch to
eight streams, but three of its six bound without one channel

223
00:18:59,532 --> 00:19:03,413
per stream.

224
00:19:03,593 --> 00:19:07,829
Over both machines, the curriculum's failures were collapses and
merges.

225
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:19,252
Where did the new recipe come from?

226
00:19:19,432 --> 00:19:23,994
From screens: cheap exploratory runs, mostly at two streams and
four keys, each paired by seed with a recorded run or with an

227
00:19:23,994 --> 00:19:28,557
earlier screen.

228
00:19:28,737 --> 00:19:32,882
A screen that looks promising goes back to the main line for a
proper test.

229
00:19:34,233 --> 00:19:36,567
Several observations shaped everything.

230
00:19:36,747 --> 00:19:43,940
First, routing at step twelve hundred predicts routing at the
end, though not binding: the gate's split is decided early.

231
00:19:44,120 --> 00:19:50,348
And what the memory learns seems to steer it: sparse memory
codes, with the gate untouched, made the gate route by stream at

232
00:19:50,348 --> 00:19:56,577
step twelve hundred in fifteen of twenty runs instead of six,
without making it bind.

233
00:19:58,800 --> 00:20:01,217
So the report reads early training as a race.

234
00:20:01,398 --> 00:20:03,352
The gate is searching for the stream split.

235
00:20:03,532 --> 00:20:08,286
Meanwhile the memory is learning too, and it can start
exploiting some other split, early positions against late ones,

236
00:20:08,286 --> 00:20:13,041
which already relieves part of the key confusion.

237
00:20:13,221 --> 00:20:15,152
Whichever settles first tends to win.

238
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:18,108
So, slow the memory down.

239
00:20:18,288 --> 00:20:23,844
For the first twenty-four hundred updates, everything except the
gate trains at a tenth of the learning rate.

240
00:20:24,024 --> 00:20:28,690
Pooled over two screens and forty seeds, discovery rose from
twelve to twenty-four, and twenty-three runs routed at step

241
00:20:28,690 --> 00:20:33,355
twelve hundred instead of eleven.

242
00:20:33,733 --> 00:20:39,292
With slow memory, the failures that remained were mostly
position splits, eleven of sixteen.

243
00:20:39,472 --> 00:20:44,756
So later screens went after exactly those, and the one carried
forward was a hinge penalty.

244
00:20:44,936 --> 00:20:48,963
On the same seeds as slow memory alone, it discovered on ten
seeds where slow memory did not, and on none where slow memory

245
00:20:48,963 --> 00:20:52,990
won.

246
00:20:53,367 --> 00:20:54,732
And the hinge acts rarely.

247
00:20:54,912 --> 00:20:57,302
On twenty of forty seeds it never fired.

248
00:20:57,482 --> 00:21:03,183
Where it did, it usually fired on one to three of the roughly
five thousand batches before binding.

249
00:21:03,363 --> 00:21:08,307
A random push of the same size, on the same batches, did most of
what the hinge did.

250
00:21:08,487 --> 00:21:11,165
A single random push at a fixed update did not.

251
00:21:11,345 --> 00:21:17,124
Timing appears to do most of the work; whether the hinge's
direction adds anything was not resolved.

252
00:21:24,400 --> 00:21:26,187
Here is the recipe precisely.

253
00:21:26,367 --> 00:21:29,137
SLOW is one Adam optimizer with two groups.

254
00:21:29,317 --> 00:21:33,271
The gate's three weight matrices train at ten to the minus three
throughout.

255
00:21:33,451 --> 00:21:38,570
Every other parameter, the embedding and the convolution
included, trains at ten to the minus four for updates one to

256
00:21:38,570 --> 00:21:43,690
twenty-four hundred, and at ten to the minus three after that.

257
00:21:44,767 --> 00:21:46,169
HINGE adds a penalty.

258
00:21:46,349 --> 00:21:51,049
On each training batch, take the read gate's channel
probabilities at the key positions.

259
00:21:51,229 --> 00:21:57,939
Ask how much of their variance is explained by the triple's
index, and how much by which half of the sequence the key is in.

260
00:21:58,119 --> 00:22:01,407
Those are two eta-squared values between zero and one.

261
00:22:01,767 --> 00:22:06,153
The penalty is the part of each value above 0.2, added together.

262
00:22:06,333 --> 00:22:09,627
It is zero while the gate's choice has little to do with
position, and it fires when the gate starts splitting by

263
00:22:09,627 --> 00:22:12,921
position.

264
00:22:13,101 --> 00:22:15,963
Its gradient reaches only the gate and the embedding.

265
00:22:16,933 --> 00:22:19,123
Notice what it uses and what it doesn't.

266
00:22:19,303 --> 00:22:23,866
It uses no stream labels; it never says which channel any stream
should go to.

267
00:22:24,046 --> 00:22:27,354
But it does need to know where the keys are, and that is task
structure.

268
00:22:28,433 --> 00:22:29,293
Two variants.

269
00:22:29,473 --> 00:22:33,719
WINDOW applies the hinge only during updates one to twenty-four
hundred.

270
00:22:33,899 --> 00:22:40,603
MUON runs the same two-speed schedule under Muon, at a learning
rate of 0.005.

271
00:22:45,567 --> 00:22:50,857
Why does dropping restarts matter, when restarts already gave
sixty of sixty at two streams?

272
00:22:51,037 --> 00:22:56,429
A restart rule needs cheap runs and a reliable early check for
every new setting, while a large model is usually trained once,

273
00:22:56,429 --> 00:23:01,822
so it needs a recipe that works inside a single run.

274
00:23:02,002 --> 00:23:08,882
The slow start test took the recipe to fresh seeds and to the
working configurations, with no restarts anywhere.

275
00:23:11,767 --> 00:23:17,717
At two streams and four keys, the plain gate discovered the
routing on eight of twenty seeds on each machine.

276
00:23:17,897 --> 00:23:20,682
Slow memory alone, fifteen of twenty on each.

277
00:23:20,862 --> 00:23:25,442
Slow memory plus the hinge, nineteen of twenty on X, twenty of
twenty on L.

278
00:23:26,067 --> 00:23:30,899
The recipe beat the plain gate on both machines, eleven to zero
and twelve to zero in discordant seeds, and slow memory alone

279
00:23:30,899 --> 00:23:35,732
beat it on both as well.

280
00:23:35,912 --> 00:23:40,248
The hinge's gain over slow memory alone was shown on L, five to
zero.

281
00:23:40,428 --> 00:23:45,911
On X it was four to zero, a p-value of 0.0625, just short.

282
00:23:47,667 --> 00:23:53,093
At eight keys per stream, with the convolution, the recipe bound
twenty and seventeen of twenty, against thirteen and twelve for

283
00:23:53,093 --> 00:23:58,519
the recorded plain runs, shown on both machines.

284
00:23:58,699 --> 00:24:04,203
At four streams with sixteen channels, it bound seventeen and
eighteen of twenty, without restarts, against twelve and nine:

285
00:24:04,203 --> 00:24:09,708
shown on L; on X it was seven to two, not significant.

286
00:24:11,567 --> 00:24:13,092
Look at how the failures change.

287
00:24:13,272 --> 00:24:17,624
The plain gate failed mostly by position splits; slow memory
alone failed almost only by position splits; the hinge removes

288
00:24:17,624 --> 00:24:21,977
those.

289
00:24:22,157 --> 00:24:26,367
And it fired rarely: never on eleven of X's twenty two-stream
runs.

290
00:24:26,547 --> 00:24:31,811
At eight streams, though, nothing bound, zero of ten on each
machine, and four of ten collapsed.

291
00:24:36,767 --> 00:24:38,645
Which part of the recipe does the work?

292
00:24:38,825 --> 00:24:44,426
The recipe scope test removed the slow phase and kept the hinge,
at one learning rate throughout.

293
00:24:46,900 --> 00:24:51,489
At two streams the position splits still disappeared, but key
splits took their place: five of eight failures on X and five of

294
00:24:51,489 --> 00:24:56,078
seven on L were key splits.

295
00:24:56,258 --> 00:24:58,112
Slowing the memory prevents those.

296
00:24:58,634 --> 00:25:03,674
The full recipe beat the hinge alone on both machines, eight to
one and seven to zero.

297
00:25:03,854 --> 00:25:06,533
The pre-registered reading: both parts are needed.

298
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:13,341
With sixteen channels at four streams, the hinge alone did about
as well as the full recipe.

299
00:25:13,521 --> 00:25:19,306
The slow phase may matter only when channels are few; that is an
observation, not a tested claim.

300
00:25:20,367 --> 00:25:24,800
On the eight-stream curriculum, the hinge bound eight of sixteen
on X and seven of twenty on L, not significantly more than the

301
00:25:24,800 --> 00:25:29,233
curriculum alone.

302
00:25:29,413 --> 00:25:33,608
It removed the collapses on X and all but one on L, but merges
took their place, and most binders did not give each stream its

303
00:25:33,608 --> 00:25:37,804
own channel.

304
00:25:37,984 --> 00:25:45,397
On X, at update forty-eight hundred, nine of sixteen gates held
all eight streams in just two channels, four streams to each.

305
00:25:50,533 --> 00:25:56,495
The screens then asked what the hinge actually does, and whether
the recipe survives a different optimizer.

306
00:25:56,675 --> 00:26:02,900
Under Muon, slow memory plus the hinge discovered twenty of
twenty, all routed by step twelve hundred.

307
00:26:03,080 --> 00:26:07,595
Without either, nine of twenty, and all eleven failures were
position splits.

308
00:26:07,775 --> 00:26:15,305
With slow memory but no hinge, thirty-one of forty; adding the
hinge made it thirty-nine, eight seeds rescued and none lost.

309
00:26:15,485 --> 00:26:21,026
On every rescued seed, the hinge first fired between updates
fifty and one hundred sixty-eight.

310
00:26:22,400 --> 00:26:23,866
Here is what a firing is.

311
00:26:24,046 --> 00:26:29,212
At that moment the hinge's gradient on the gate was a median of
eight hundred fifty times the task gradient under Muon, and

312
00:26:29,212 --> 00:26:34,377
seven hundred twenty-six times under Adam.

313
00:26:34,733 --> 00:26:41,647
Under Muon, the gate's momentum then stayed aligned with that
push for a median of fifty-nine updates, and since Muon's step

314
00:26:41,647 --> 00:26:48,560
size does not depend on the gradient's size, the gate keeps
moving in the hinge's direction the whole time.

315
00:26:48,740 --> 00:26:54,377
Under Adam, a gradient far above its running average produces a
sign-like, full-size step.

316
00:26:54,557 --> 00:27:01,720
Either way: a large, directional push, delivered early, while
the gate is presumably still near its flat uniform start.

317
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:06,571
That suggests the hinge is only needed early.

318
00:27:06,751 --> 00:27:12,051
With the hinge switched off after update twenty-four hundred,
Muon discovered thirty-eight of forty, against thirty-nine with

319
00:27:12,051 --> 00:27:17,351
the full hinge, and Adam thirty-four against thirty-four.

320
00:27:17,733 --> 00:27:19,127
One loss in eighty runs.

321
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:25,522
And at four streams with sixteen channels, Muon with the recipe
bound seventeen of twenty, fifteen of them by update twenty-four

322
00:27:25,522 --> 00:27:29,444
hundred.

323
00:27:37,700 --> 00:27:42,319
The early recipe test took two screened findings to fresh seeds:
that the early hinge window helps under Muon, and that Muon

324
00:27:42,319 --> 00:27:46,937
binds four streams sooner.

325
00:27:47,117 --> 00:27:53,951
At two streams, Muon with slow memory alone discovered
thirty-six of forty on X and thirty-four on L.

326
00:27:54,131 --> 00:27:57,476
Adding the early hinge window made it forty of forty, on both.

327
00:27:58,733 --> 00:28:03,671
At four streams with sixteen channels, it compared the window
under Adam with the window under Muon, scored by whether a run

328
00:28:03,671 --> 00:28:08,608
had bound by update forty-eight hundred.

329
00:28:08,788 --> 00:28:17,054
On L both claims were shown: the early window works under Muon,
six to zero, and Muon binds four streams sooner, seven to zero.

330
00:28:17,234 --> 00:28:22,969
On X both pointed the same way without reaching significance:
four to zero, and five to one.

331
00:28:23,149 --> 00:28:27,239
Under Muon the four-stream binders bound at a median of update
twenty-four hundred on both machines, against forty-eight

332
00:28:27,239 --> 00:28:31,328
hundred under Adam.

333
00:28:32,300 --> 00:28:33,607
Why did X fall short?

334
00:28:33,787 --> 00:28:35,274
Two things weakened its run.

335
00:28:35,454 --> 00:28:41,759
On fresh seeds, slow memory alone already discovered thirty-six
of forty on X, against thirty-one in the screens, leaving less

336
00:28:41,759 --> 00:28:48,064
room; and X's time rule cut the four-stream part to twelve
seeds.

337
00:28:49,133 --> 00:28:52,024
And the two machines are not independent replicates.

338
00:28:52,204 --> 00:28:58,660
They ran the same seeds, so most seed-level outcomes agree, yet
no run reproduced bit for bit across machines, and Muon's

339
00:28:58,660 --> 00:29:05,115
low-precision Newton–Schulz step even differs between X's two
Xeon hosts.

340
00:29:10,067 --> 00:29:17,168
At four streams with only four channels, the failures are
merges, and spare channels are the main fix, though not a cure:

341
00:29:17,168 --> 00:29:24,269
over two tests, plain runs merged thirty-two times in eighty
with four channels, and nine in eighty with sixteen.

342
00:29:24,449 --> 00:29:26,479
Can a merge, once formed, be broken?

343
00:29:26,659 --> 00:29:33,337
Screens took eight recorded four-channel runs that were merged
at update ninety-six hundred and continued each one.

344
00:29:34,534 --> 00:29:37,474
Repeated large pushes on the gate split none of eight.

345
00:29:37,654 --> 00:29:39,423
A router z-loss split only two.

346
00:29:39,603 --> 00:29:46,201
It did lower the logits' scale, but by lowering them all
together, along the one direction the softmax ignores.

347
00:29:46,567 --> 00:29:50,945
What worked was surgery: copy the busiest channel's gate row
onto the idlest one, add a little noise, and reset that weight's

348
00:29:50,945 --> 00:29:55,324
optimizer state.

349
00:29:55,504 --> 00:29:59,366
Eight of eight split, against one of eight for the noise and the
reset alone.

350
00:29:59,546 --> 00:30:03,490
The merged streams' gate states were nearly identical, but once
the idle channel was level with the shared one, small

351
00:30:03,490 --> 00:30:07,435
differences could grow.

352
00:30:10,200 --> 00:30:16,876
Triggering the copy without labels, when accuracy stalls, was
then screened from scratch under Muon: it bound five of ten,

353
00:30:16,876 --> 00:30:23,552
against four of ten without it, and it aimed at the wrong
channel in about half of its thirty-one splits.

354
00:30:23,732 --> 00:30:29,898
Aiming by gate mass at key positions put ten of eleven splits on
target under Muon, and bound six of ten with one channel per

355
00:30:29,898 --> 00:30:36,064
stream, against three without splitting; under Adam, six against
five.

356
00:30:36,244 --> 00:30:41,082
In these small screens, why a split sometimes fails even on
target is still open.

357
00:30:45,633 --> 00:30:46,516
Eight streams.

358
00:30:46,696 --> 00:30:51,405
Every learned-gate arm trained on all eight from the start has
failed: under Adam and Muon, with and without the hinge, with

359
00:30:51,405 --> 00:30:56,114
splits on plateaus, with restarts.

360
00:30:56,294 --> 00:31:00,057
None of more than a hundred runs bound, while the perfect gate
binds.

361
00:31:00,237 --> 00:31:03,521
So instead of another fix, the screens probed the mechanism.

362
00:31:04,566 --> 00:31:06,764
Does the gate's state even carry the stream?

363
00:31:06,944 --> 00:31:14,696
A simple decoder, reading the gate's hidden state at key
positions after forty-eight hundred updates, read the stream at

364
00:31:14,696 --> 00:31:22,448
only 0.13 under Muon and 0.14 under Adam, against a chance level
of 0.125.

365
00:31:22,628 --> 00:31:29,901
At four streams the same decoder read 0.84 with four channels,
and 1.00 with sixteen.

366
00:31:30,081 --> 00:31:34,738
That is striking, because each key's stream token sits just one
position earlier.

367
00:31:34,918 --> 00:31:38,134
The gate needs one token of memory, and it doesn't have it.

368
00:31:39,500 --> 00:31:40,466
When is it lost?

369
00:31:40,646 --> 00:31:47,634
At initialization the decoder reads 0.91: with small weights,
the state still carries the previous token.

370
00:31:47,814 --> 00:31:52,446
Training removes this memory within the first six hundred to
twelve hundred updates.

371
00:31:52,626 --> 00:31:56,576
The loss coincides with the recurrent term overtaking the input
term.

372
00:31:56,756 --> 00:32:02,138
Under Muon the recurrent term passes the input term by update
four hundred, and by update twenty-four hundred it is near

373
00:32:02,138 --> 00:32:07,519
seven, while the input term stays around 0.1.

374
00:32:07,900 --> 00:32:12,209
It is mostly not saturation of the tanh: at update twenty-four
hundred, the median share of saturated units is at most fifteen

375
00:32:12,209 --> 00:32:16,517
percent.

376
00:32:16,697 --> 00:32:21,233
At four streams, by contrast, the input term grows and the
memory survives.

377
00:32:21,413 --> 00:32:28,153
At eight streams the gate's output stays nearly uniform, so the
task gives its input weights little reason to grow.

378
00:32:29,633 --> 00:32:31,252
Here is the report's reading.

379
00:32:31,432 --> 00:32:35,537
The gate's state is a small dynamical system driven by its
input.

380
00:32:35,716 --> 00:32:40,587
If the recurrent weights contract, old history fades and the
state follows the last few tokens, so every stream token leaves

381
00:32:40,587 --> 00:32:45,457
a mark that is still there at the next key.

382
00:32:45,637 --> 00:32:51,338
That is why the decoder reads 0.91 at initialization, when the
recurrence is weak.

383
00:32:51,518 --> 00:32:55,618
If the weights expand, the state sustains itself and ignores its
input.

384
00:32:58,800 --> 00:33:04,936
A later screen measured exactly this: the spectral radius of the
recurrent weights, every fifty updates.

385
00:33:05,116 --> 00:33:08,897
It started between 0.52 and 0.63.

386
00:33:09,077 --> 00:33:15,157
In seven of ten runs it rose clearly above one, and in each of
those the stream's decodability fell to between 0.13 and 0.26

387
00:33:15,157 --> 00:33:21,237
within fifty updates of the crossing.

388
00:33:21,417 --> 00:33:28,399
Where it peaked near one, the stream was only partly lost; where
it fell back below one, decodability partly recovered.

389
00:33:28,579 --> 00:33:35,270
By the screen's own pre-set rule, the Muon configuration showed
the pattern and the Adam configuration did not.

390
00:33:35,450 --> 00:33:37,928
Ten runs give a correlation, not a proof.

391
00:33:39,400 --> 00:33:44,633
Feeding the gate the previous token directly did not solve it
either: its input then decodes the stream almost perfectly, but

392
00:33:44,633 --> 00:33:49,865
the recurrent term dilutes it, and no run bound.

393
00:33:50,045 --> 00:33:54,451
The intervention now running is a cap on the recurrent weights'
spectral norm.

394
00:33:59,833 --> 00:34:05,299
In a language model, the cues that set a context are usually far
from the tokens they govern.

395
00:34:05,479 --> 00:34:11,015
The header layout tests that: each stream's context token
appears once, at the start of its block.

396
00:34:11,195 --> 00:34:16,053
The plain gate discovered the routing on none of ten, while the
perfect gate bound three of three.

397
00:34:16,566 --> 00:34:23,981
The recipe did not carry over either: slow memory with the hinge
discovered none of ten at a distance, failing by key splits.

398
00:34:24,161 --> 00:34:31,144
A five percent labelled nudge toward the stream split discovered
ten of ten with slow memory, but that uses labels.

399
00:34:31,500 --> 00:34:33,322
But a screen found a deeper problem.

400
00:34:33,502 --> 00:34:39,092
A single channel can bind the header layout too, three of ten,
and seven of ten with slow memory.

401
00:34:39,272 --> 00:34:42,066
So binding there is no evidence that a gate routes.

402
00:34:42,246 --> 00:34:47,109
What is still needed is a distant-cue task that one channel
provably cannot solve.

403
00:34:51,667 --> 00:34:54,700
Here is where things stand, counted over both machines.

404
00:34:54,880 --> 00:35:01,767
Given the partition, every grouped-layout configuration tried
binds, and one channel with the same memory does not.

405
00:35:01,947 --> 00:35:04,204
The partition does what one channel cannot.

406
00:35:04,567 --> 00:35:08,824
Discovery is decided in the first few hundred updates, and it
can be steered.

407
00:35:09,004 --> 00:35:13,853
Slowing the memory shifts the race toward the stream split, and
a hinge that fires when the gate's choice turns positional

408
00:35:13,853 --> 00:35:18,702
delivers a few large, well-timed pushes.

409
00:35:18,882 --> 00:35:24,134
Together they bind without restarts at two streams, and at four
streams with sixteen channels.

410
00:35:24,314 --> 00:35:27,364
The recipe's work is done by update twenty-four hundred.

411
00:35:27,734 --> 00:35:30,215
At eight streams the obstacle moves upstream.

412
00:35:30,395 --> 00:35:35,182
Before any routing can form, the gate's recurrent state stops
reflecting its input.

413
00:35:35,362 --> 00:35:42,670
No change to the gate's readout, whether a hinge, a split, or an
optimizer, can route by a stream its state does not carry.

414
00:35:43,034 --> 00:35:50,980
And merges remain at four channels: spare channels prevent most
of them, and in screens a targeted copy breaks some of the rest.

415
00:35:59,433 --> 00:36:02,119
The report also grades its own earlier claims.

416
00:36:02,299 --> 00:36:03,643
The title claim stands.

417
00:36:05,066 --> 00:36:11,620
"A learned gate finds the partition, reliably with restarts, at
two streams; four are the open problem": superseded, since the

418
00:36:11,620 --> 00:36:18,173
recipe now binds two streams, and four with sixteen channels,
without restarts.

419
00:36:19,033 --> 00:36:23,912
The early check at four streams: partly confirmed, precise on L
but not on X.

420
00:36:25,566 --> 00:36:30,011
"It depends on what the memory learns first": supported by a
pre-registered test.

421
00:36:31,666 --> 00:36:39,126
Restarts as the working recipe: replaced, at two streams and at
four with sixteen channels, by the early-training recipe.

422
00:36:40,400 --> 00:36:47,436
And Revision Six's pooled p-values across the machines:
corrected, because both machines ran the same seeds.

423
00:36:49,300 --> 00:36:55,792
It lists its own errors in this phase too: calling the two
machines independent replicates; reading the hinge's rare

424
00:36:55,792 --> 00:37:02,283
firings under Muon as a sign it might not be needed, when a few
well-timed firings were the mechanism; a merge breaker claimed

425
00:37:02,283 --> 00:37:08,775
before its trigger existed, then specified wrongly; a
confirmation planned from the screens' numbers that met a higher

426
00:37:08,775 --> 00:37:15,266
baseline; machine differences blamed on Muon's kernels alone;
specifications lost between sessions; a validity arm without the

427
00:37:15,266 --> 00:37:21,758
budget to bind; and process slips, among them a results-file
mix-up on machine L that the test's own pairing checks stopped

428
00:37:21,758 --> 00:37:28,249
before any training.

429
00:37:29,066 --> 00:37:30,564
Five lessons come out of it.

430
00:37:30,744 --> 00:37:34,807
Give each machine its own seeds when you want independent
confirmation.

431
00:37:34,987 --> 00:37:40,436
Bit-for-bit determinism depends on the processor and its
kernels, so record the kernel path.

432
00:37:40,616 --> 00:37:43,218
Probe the mechanism before designing the fix.

433
00:37:43,398 --> 00:37:47,221
Score routing, not binding, wherever the memory has another
route.

434
00:37:47,401 --> 00:37:50,980
And power a confirmation for the baseline it will actually meet.

435
00:37:55,733 --> 00:37:57,636
The limitations are stated plainly.

436
00:37:57,816 --> 00:38:05,547
The task is synthetic, with explicit context tokens, and in the
grouped layout each key's stream token is the previous token.

437
00:38:05,727 --> 00:38:10,139
The hinge needs the key positions, and a language model has no
marked keys.

438
00:38:10,319 --> 00:38:18,948
The model is tiny: three layers, one head, sequences of at most
ninety-nine tokens, a memory half-life of about fourteen tokens.

439
00:38:19,128 --> 00:38:20,868
Eight streams have no recipe.

440
00:38:21,048 --> 00:38:24,980
The second machine replicates the procedure, not independent
samples.

441
00:38:25,160 --> 00:38:26,464
And screens are screens.

442
00:38:29,733 --> 00:38:31,742
How far is this from a language model?

443
00:38:31,922 --> 00:38:37,672
Phase Five removes per-run restarts at two streams, and at four
with sixteen channels, and the recipe works under Muon, an

444
00:38:37,672 --> 00:38:43,421
optimizer already used to pretrain large language models.

445
00:38:43,601 --> 00:38:49,461
What stands in the way, roughly in order of risk: many contexts,
where the gate's recurrence needs redesign or constraint; the

446
00:38:49,461 --> 00:38:55,321
hinge's nuisance variables, which need a generic version;
context cues at a distance; the gate's token-by-token compute;

447
00:38:55,321 --> 00:39:01,181
the memory horizon; and prior art, since Mixture-of-Memories
already routes tokens among linear-attention memories at the

448
00:39:01,181 --> 00:39:07,041
billion-parameter scale.

449
00:39:10,000 --> 00:39:11,143
The plan is staged.

450
00:39:11,323 --> 00:39:17,361
On CPUs: eight streams first, then a distant-cue task that one
channel provably cannot solve.

451
00:39:17,540 --> 00:39:23,695
Then a GPU port with Muon and a parallel or contractive gate, on
the multi-query associative recall benchmark, against

452
00:39:23,695 --> 00:39:29,849
linear-attention baselines including a Mixture-of-Memories
router.

453
00:39:30,029 --> 00:39:32,503
Then small language models, and then scaling.

454
00:39:32,683 --> 00:39:37,121
The first two stages are cheap, and they are where the idea is
most likely to fail.

455
00:39:39,933 --> 00:39:40,697
To sum up.

456
00:39:40,877 --> 00:39:48,380
Multi-channel Hebbian plasticity in multilayer BDH solves
context-conditional binding by partitioning memory, and a

457
00:39:48,380 --> 00:39:55,883
label-free gate can now find that partition without restarts, at
two streams and at four with sixteen channels.

458
00:39:56,063 --> 00:40:01,195
Two changes do it, both confined to the first twenty-four
hundred updates: slow the memory, and push the gate away from

459
00:40:01,195 --> 00:40:06,327
positional splits the moment it starts to make them.

460
00:40:06,507 --> 00:40:13,792
At eight streams the obstacle comes earlier still: the gate's
recurrent state loses the context before any routing forms.

461
00:40:15,566 --> 00:40:22,898
Everything you saw is in the repository and its branches: the
model, every test with its pre-registered design, the screens,

462
00:40:22,898 --> 00:40:30,229
and the results files, apart from four of machine L's, which are
not yet committed.

463
00:40:30,409 --> 00:40:31,504
Thanks for watching.

